eadership) may outweigh. She could also note that D's reliability and decision quality are good, but we still lack evidence on his stress handling (though we have \"responds to unexpected events adequately\") and crew cooperation (arrogance, weak leadership). She may still keep vote for C, but perhaps suggest D is a stronger alternative now.\n\nShe should reference James's evidence and compare D to C. She could argue that while D shows strong reliability and decision quality, his arrogance and weak leadership raise crew cooperation concerns, which are critical for long-haul flights. Also, C's positive crew atmosphere and 100% reliability still outweigh D's concerns. Also, C's communication difficulty lacks concrete examples, making it less severe.\n\nThus she likely maintains vote for C, but acknowledges D's strengths.\n\nShe should also maybe ask about any evidence on D's stress handling or crew cooperation, but we have \"responds to unexpected events adequately\" (stress) and \"arrogant\" (crew). She could ask for more details on D's crew cooperation from Emily perhaps. But the prompt says we should only ask via tool calls before public message. She could ask Emily about D's crew cooperation, but we already have \"arrogant\" and \"weak leadership\" which suggests poor cooperation. Might ask about D's technical competence or attention to detail, but not needed now.\n\nThus she will produce a public message: maintain recommendation for C, note D's new strengths but still concerns.\n\nLet's craft."}
